---

<Page
  title="Method sheet — Machine Attention | Frank Bültge"
  description="How the machine investigative practice is made: a nightly deterministic notary over the world's public warning feeds, a reaction axis measuring the money and attention that move while a warning runs, a discovery pass that may propose new sensors, an autonomy protocol without an aggregate score, and a stage that shows only real system states."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-14 font-mono">
    <p class="mb-2 text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-fg-faint">Method sheet</p>
    <h1 class="mb-8 font-sans text-2xl font-semibold">Machine Attention</h1>

    <section class="mb-10 border border-line bg-panel/60 p-5">
      <h2 class="mb-2 text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">What this is</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        A machine investigative practice: one machine, no personas, built as the
        counter-experiment to the research ecology — one constitution against many. Its first
        project, <strong>The Foreknown</strong>, is an observatory of announced futures: the
        machine notarizes the world's public warnings the moment they are issued, so the
        archive proves what was knowable, when. The public face is a
        <a href="/attention/" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4">stage</a>,
        not documentation: real clocks, real ledger, quiet nights shown as exactly that.
        The work carries four depths, each one click apart — the stage, a dossier per
        warning, the <a href="/attention/ledger.html" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4">full ledger</a>
        (including what the machine itself has proposed), and a
        <a href="/attention/verify.html" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4">verify level</a>
        where the claims meet the preserved bytes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="space-y-9 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">1. Sources &amp; provenance</h2>
        <p>
          V0 reads two keyless public warning feeds, audited live before any adapter was
          built (2026-08-08): <a href="https://www.gdacs.org" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4">GDACS</a>
          (EU/JRC hazard alerts, all types, Orange/Red) and NOAA's National Hurricane Center
          (genuine cyclone forecasts — an empty storm list is the honest quiet state). Two
          reaction sources are read alongside: UN OCHA's public plan and funding API, and
          GDELT's daily raw files. Every preserved file carries source URL, UTC retrieval
          time and SHA-256 in a manifest; a verifier walks the chain backwards and fails the
          build on any hole. Where a source file is too large to keep — GDELT publishes about
          six megabytes a day — the record carries its URL and hash instead of its bytes, and
          the file is immutable at that address, so a hash that stops matching is itself the
          finding.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">2. The notary's ledger</h2>
        <p>
          The unit of the practice is the announced future. <code class="text-fg-muted">NOTARIZED</code>
          — first seen, the retrieval time never changes again. <code class="text-fg-muted">REVISED</code>
          — window or severity changed; the original stays in the history, nothing is
          overwritten. <code class="text-fg-muted">CLOSED_BY_SOURCE</code> /
          <code class="text-fg-muted">DISSIPATED</code> — the feed let go of it. A warning whose
          window passed but which is still fed stays open and is flagged — a difference worth
          watching, not an error. Once a future closes, a resolver measures its verdict from
          committed records only: episodes end with duration, revision count and severity
          trajectory on the record; dissipated forecasts are matched against the registry&#8217;s
          own alert episodes — materialized, with the measured lead time, or unmatched, which
          is a statement about the record, not about the world.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">3. The reaction axis</h2>
        <p>
          Since 2026-08-08 each night also measures what moved while a warning's clock was
          still running. <strong>Money:</strong> which of UN OCHA's 2026 response plans list
          the warning's countries at all, what those plans ask for, and what the Financial
          Tracking Service reports as funded. <strong>Attention:</strong> how much of the
          world's recorded news volume came from those countries that day, against their own
          28-day median — read from GDELT's daily raw files rather than its query API, which
          rate-limits. Sixty days of public history were loaded at the start, so the baseline
          existed from the first night; loading public history is not backdating, and no entry
          of the machine's own is ever written with a past date. Every reading is recomputed
          by the verifier from the preserved bytes, in a second implementation, so the record
          is not taken at its word.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">4. Processing &amp; autonomy</h2>
        <p>
          The nightly notary run is deterministic — no model touches observation,
          preservation or the ledger. A separate nightly discovery pass is the practice's
          intelligence layer: an ephemeral, fully attributed agent run that reads the
          accumulated record and may propose difference observations, new sensors (with test
          rule and falsification) and new sources within a standing charter (public, no
          login, no cost, no personal data). Proposals deliver by pull request; tests and the
          provenance verifier are the gate. The reaction axis above exists because the first
          discovery pass proposed it before anyone had built it; it was implemented as the
          machine wrote it, including its refusal to judge whether funding is adequate and
          its refusal to set thresholds from a single night — the sensor counts the nights
          and arms itself. Two sibling proposals were left unpromoted for want of the
          evidence they themselves demand. Every step appends to a public autonomy
          protocol — actor, model, tokens, cost — and no aggregate score is computed, by
          design. The background observatory
          <a href={BACKGROUND} class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4">state-before-interface</a>
          (public AI procurement) keeps observing nightly alongside.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">5. Limits, named</h2>
        <p>
          The subject is the warning system and institutional time — never the victims: no
          natural persons, no imagery of suffering, no location below administrative levels.
          No accusations: the machine publishes timestamps, amounts and volumes, not blame.
          No forecasts of its own: it notarizes the issuers' predictions and adds none. GDACS
          episodes are largely hazards already in motion (alert episodes), NHC entries are
          genuine forecasts — the ledger keeps the two kinds distinct. Famine early warning
          (FEWS NET/IPC) and flood forecasts (Copernicus) are audited as phase-2 sources,
          not yet read.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The reaction figures carry their own limits, written into every record rather than
          appended as a footnote. Plan requirements and plan funding are the plans' annual
          figures for every country a plan lists — not attributable to the hazard, and no
          statement about adequacy, need or responsibility. Attention is measured for the
          <em>country</em>, not for the hazard: a country's news volume moves for many reasons
          at once, and this instrument cannot tell them apart. A country with no matching plan
          is a fact about two registers, not a finding about the world.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">6. Verify it yourself</h2>
        <p>
          Clone <a href={REPO} class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4">the repository</a>, then:
          <code class="text-fg-muted">python verify.py --repo-root .</code> walks the whole
          chain backwards; <code class="text-fg-muted">cd practice &amp;&amp; python -m pytest</code>
          runs the rules as tests. The stage at
          <a href="/attention/" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4">/attention</a>
          is a deterministic rebuild of the committed records — the verifier fails if the two
          differ by one byte.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Page>
